│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ hexagonal_rows_grow_radii                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The current constructor uses a symmetric ring layout which wastes space and a flawed      │
│                          │ one-pass radius scaling. I replace it with a hexagonal-lattice layout: 6 rows with counts │
│                          │ [5,4,5,4,5,3] = 26 circles. With touching neighbors (dx = 2r, dy = √3·r), the binding     │
│                          │ constraints are horizontal (2r + 4dx = 10r ≤ 1) and vertical (2r + 5√3·r ≤ 1), giving r ≈ │
│                          │ 0.0938 and a uniform sum of ~2.44 (vs. current 0.96). Then, instead of a one-pass         │
│                          │ pairwise shrink (which underestimates radii), I add a Gauss–Seidel *growth* refinement:   │
│                          │ each circle's radius is grown up to the minimum of its wall distance and (distance to     │
│                          │ neighbor − neighbor's radius), iterating until no circle can grow. This lets circles near │
│                          │ walls and in the truncated last row expand into unused space, pushing the sum toward the  │
│                          │ 2.6+ regime while guaranteeing feasibility (radii only grow when locally valid, boundary  │
│                          │ distances are hard caps).                                                                 │
