│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ hex_rows_greedy_expansion                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ Replace the ad-hoc concentric-ring layout (which only achieves sum ≈ 0.96) with a proper  │
│                          │ hexagonal lattice arrangement: rows of 5,4,5,4,5,3 circles with hexagonal vertical        │
│                          │ spacing (√3·r) and staggered columns — the classic densest local structure for circle     │
│                          │ packing. With 6 rows, the equal radius that fits the unit square is r ≈ 1/(2+5√3) ≈       │
│                          │ 0.0939, giving sum ≈ 2.44 immediately. Then, because square corners leave extra space     │
│                          │ that a pure hex lattice wastes, I add a greedy expansion phase: repeatedly sweep over     │
│                          │ circles and grow each one to the maximum radius allowed by the walls and its current      │
│                          │ neighbors (min over wall distances and d_ij - r_j). This is always feasible (each step    │
│                          │ keeps the packing valid) and monotonically increases the sum, letting corner/edge circles │
│                          │ bulge into the leftover corner space — moving the sum toward the 2.635 AlphaEvolve        │
│                          │ target.                                                                                   │
