│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ hex_grid_5x5_plus_gap_circle                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ Replace the naive concentric-ring layout with a near-optimal explicit construction: a 5x5 │
│                          │ grid of circles of radius 0.1 (spacing 0.2, tangent horizontally/vertically, exactly      │
│                          │ fitting the unit square), plus a 26th small circle tucked into a cell-corner gap. Each    │
│                          │ grid circle has radius exactly 0.1 (border-limited, spacing exactly 2r), giving 2.5, and  │
│                          │ the extra circle at (0.2,0.2) has radius 0.2*sqrt(2)/2 - 0.1 ≈ 0.0414 (limited by its     │
│                          │ diagonal neighbors), giving a total of ~2.5414 — a large improvement over the current     │
│                          │ 0.96. The radius computation is also fixed: the old pairwise proportional scaling         │
│                          │ incorrectly shrank already-valid circles. Instead, radii are computed by iterative        │
│                          │ constraint propagation: start each radius at its border limit and repeatedly enforce r_i  │
│                          │ + r_j <= d_ij by shrinking only the violating circle, which converges to the maximal      │
│                          │ radii without degrading valid ones.                                                       │
│ num_applied              │ 2                                                                                         │
