│ patch_description        │ The best prior program (score 1.60, sum_radii 1.60) combined lattice enumeration with     │
│                          │ uniform-radius repulsion. The key remaining inefficiency is that uniform-radius           │
│                          │ relaxation treats all circles identically, while in an optimal packing of unequal         │
│                          │ circles, small circles should be pushed against walls while large circles occupy the      │
│                          │ interior. I propose a second refinement stage: a variable-radius relaxation where each    │
│                          │ circle's own grown radius (from compute_max_radii) determines its target spacing, plus an │
│                          │ adaptive wall-pull term. Each iteration: (1) compute each circle's current radius; (2)    │
│                          │ push apart pairs whose distance is less than the sum of their desired radii (weighted     │
│                          │ per-pair, not uniform r0); (3) for each circle, compare its wall-limited radius with its  │
│                          │ neighbor-limited radius — if the neighbor constraint is tighter, the circle has room      │
│                          │ toward the wall, so pull it toward its nearest wall with a step proportional to its       │
│                          │ radius deficit. This per-circle adaptivity is exactly the mechanism that lets boundary    │
│                          │ circles shrink and hug walls while interior circles expand, moving toward the 2.635       │
│                          │ target.                                                                                   │
