│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ add_slsqp_gradient_refinement                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ The greedy push heuristics plateau at 2.42 because each circle only moves in crude        │
│                          │ discrete directions. A proper continuous optimizer can do much better. I add an SLSQP     │
│                          │ (scipy) refinement stage that directly maximizes the sum of radii as a smooth function of │
│                          │ the circle centers: at any fixed center configuration, the optimal radii are given by the │
│                          │ exact Gauss-Seidel growth, and the objective is differentiable almost everywhere. I       │
│                          │ parameterize variables as the 2n center coordinates, and compute radii via a fast inner   │
│                          │ fixed-point loop (a smooth soft-min can be used, but the fixed point of the hard max is   │
│                          │ what we want; SLSQP handles the nonsmoothness reasonably if we instead use a smooth       │
│                          │ log-sum-exp lower bound with inequality constraints r_i + r_j <= d_ij and r_i <= wall).   │
│                          │ Concretely: variables = (x, y, r) for all circles (3n = 78 vars), objective = -sum(r),    │
│                          │ constraints: r_i + r_j <= dist for all pairs, and r_i <= min-distance-to-walls. This is a │
│                          │ standard formulation of the packing problem and SLSQP handles it well locally, given a    │
│                          │ feasible starting point from the current best. We run it on the top few candidates and    │
│                          │ the final result, keeping the best valid output. Validity is preserved because we shrink  │
│                          │ radii at the end by a small safety margin and re-verify with the exact grow_radii, which  │
│                          │ is always feasible by construction. To keep eval time bounded, we use the top 3 seeds     │
│                          │ plus the void-filled result, with a limited iteration budget.                             │
